# build output (idf.py -B <dir>); sdkconfig.release / sdkconfig.encrypted are
# regenerated by tools/build_release.sh / tools/build_encrypted_release.sh
/build*/
main/build-disp/
# the offline installer zip: derived from docs/ and the release build, attached
# to the GitHub Release rather than carried in git history
/dist/
sdkconfig.release
sdkconfig.encrypted
sdkconfig.encrypted-rehearsal
sdkconfig.old

# IDF component manager downloads these from idf_component.yml +
# dependencies.lock (which IS tracked)
managed_components/
managed_components

# local tooling / OS noise
tools/fonts/node_modules/
.DS_Store
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# design handoff bundles: reference material unzipped into the repo root while
# the work is being done -- a brief, a .dc.html canvas and a screenshot of what
# is being replaced. Never committed, the repo carries the results rather than
# the brief. The SETTINGS one is the worked example: its five section tabs
# shipped in b690465, and the folder that described them adds nothing the code
# does not already say.
design_handoff_*/

# third party audit working notes (crypto-wallet-security-audit skill): working
# state, not a deliverable. Only a reviewed summary belongs in the repo.
audit-notes/
Sign IO Visualization.dc.html

# Signing keys. The device refuses an OTA image whose signature does not check
# against the public key baked into it, so the private half of that pair is the
# one file in this project that can make firmware your signer will boot. It
# lives in ~/.kiss-signer/ and is never in the tree -- build_release.sh reads it
# from there, and docs/installer/SIGNING.md says to keep it offline like the GPG
# key. A stray test key was sitting in the repo root; one `git add -A` is all
# that stands between that and a public repo, which is why this is a pattern and
# not a filename.
*.pem
!docs/installer/kiss_ota_pub.pem

# The sdtest board config, same as its release/encrypted siblings above.
sdkconfig.sdtest

# Local editor and tool settings. Machine specific, and the repository is not
# where they belong: .claude/settings.local.json was tracked for eight commits
# before anyone noticed.
.claude/

# offline fixture tooling (tools/sign_fixtures/README names the pip install)
venv/

# The working rules for whoever is at the keyboard. Not a user document, and
# the one tracked file that named a tool; it stays on disk and out of the tree.
CLAUDE.md
